---
import PageSection from "../../components/PageSection.astro";
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { copy } from "../../lib/i18n";

const lang = "en";
const text = copy[lang];

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/",
        name: "Lilo Browser",
        position: 1,
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        item: "https://getlilo.app/compare/",
        name: "Compare browsers",
        position: 2,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://getlilo.app/compare/#faq",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    inLanguage: "en",
    mainEntity: [],
    url: "https://getlilo.app/compare/",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  lang={lang}
  title="Lilo vs Safari, Zen, Orion and Dia: Mac browsers with a sidebar, compared"
  description="How Lilo Browser compares with Safari, Zen, Orion and Dia on the Mac: engine, source code, price, sidebar tabs, extensions and platforms, and when another browser fits you better."
  schema={schema}
  hero={{
    heading: text.compareHeading,
    description: text.compareIntroduction,
    tag: text.compare,
    link: { href: "#table", label: text.compareHeroLink },
  }}
  closing={{
    heading: text.compareClosingHeading,
    description: text.compareClosingDescription,
  }}
>
  <PageSection id="table">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Side by side</span>
      <h2 id="browser-comparison">Five browsers at a glance.</h2>
      <p>
        Checked against each project's own pages in October 2026. Browsers
        change fast:{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/issues">tell us</a> if
        something here is out of date.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="matrix"
      role="region"
      aria-labelledby="browser-comparison"
      tabindex="0"
    >
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <td></td>
            <th scope="col">Lilo</th>
            <th scope="col">Safari</th>
            <th scope="col">Zen</th>
            <th scope="col">Orion</th>
            <th scope="col">Dia</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Engine</th>
            <td>WebKit</td>
            <td>WebKit</td>
            <td>Gecko, from Firefox</td>
            <td>WebKit</td>
            <td>Chromium</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Made by</th>
            <td>Alexays</td>
            <td>Apple</td>
            <td>Zen's open-source team</td>
            <td>Kagi</td>
            <td>The Browser Company, Atlassian</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Source code</th>
            <td class="y">Open source, MIT</td>
            <td>Closed app, open engine</td>
            <td class="y">Open source, MPL 2.0</td>
            <td>Closed Mac app</td>
            <td class="n">Closed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Price</th>
            <td>Free</td>
            <td>Free</td>
            <td>Free</td>
            <td>Free, paid Orion+ tier</td>
            <td>Free, paid Pro tier</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Tabs in a sidebar</th>
            <td class="y">Yes, or on any edge</td>
            <td class="n">No, tabs in a row</td>
            <td class="y">Yes</td>
            <td class="y">Yes, as an option</td>
            <td class="y">Yes, as an option</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Extensions</th>
            <td>Chrome Web Store, Manifest V3</td>
            <td>Safari extensions</td>
            <td>Firefox add-ons</td>
            <td>Many Chrome and Firefox ones</td>
            <td>Chrome Web Store</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Platforms</th>
            <td>Mac, macOS 26 or later</td>
            <td>Mac, iPhone, iPad</td>
            <td>Mac, Windows, Linux</td>
            <td>Mac, iPhone, iPad</td>
            <td>Mac</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="lilo">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">What only Lilo does</span>
      <h2>A native Mac browser for people who live in a sidebar.</h2>
      <p>
        These are the parts you will not find together anywhere else in the
        table.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>WebKit with Chrome extensions.</b> The engine Safari uses, and the
        Chrome Web Store's Manifest V3 extensions installed in one click.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Spaces with their own profiles.</b> Each Space can keep its own
        sign-ins and extensions, in one window, with tabs on any edge.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Arc's power features.</b> Peek, a Little window from any app, nested
        folders, an auto-archive, and link rules like Air Traffic Control.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Terminal tabs.</b> A real terminal beside your pages, with each
        repository's branch and pending changes in the sidebar.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>A browser for your AI agents.</b> No AI of its own, but a local MCP
        server so Claude Code, Codex or Cursor browse in their own Space, and
        Lilo asks you before they send, pay or delete.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Nothing to switch off.</b> MIT licensed, no account, no telemetry and
        no server.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="safari">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Lilo or Safari</span>
      <h2>Same engine, a different way to work.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>Choose Safari</b> if you want your tabs and history on your iPhone,
        Apple Pay on websites, or passkeys from iCloud Keychain, which Lilo
        cannot use until Apple grants it access.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Choose Lilo</b> if you want Spaces in a sidebar, Chrome extensions,
        Peek and link rules, on the same WebKit engine. Lilo imports your Safari
        bookmarks, tabs and history.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="zen">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Lilo or Zen</span>
      <h2>Two open-source takes on Arc.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>Choose Zen</b> if you also use Windows or Linux, want Firefox's
        engine and add-ons, or need your Spaces synced between computers.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Choose Lilo</b> if you are on a Mac and want a native app on WebKit
        that runs Chrome extensions, with terminal tabs and an MCP server for
        your agents. Lilo imports from Zen too.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="orion">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Lilo or Orion</span>
      <h2>Two WebKit browsers that run other browsers' extensions.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>Choose Orion</b> if you want the same browser on your iPhone and
        iPad, or Firefox extensions as well as Chrome ones.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Choose Lilo</b> if you want open-source code, Arc-style Spaces with
        their own profiles, an Arc import, terminal tabs and a browser your AI
        agents can drive.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="dia">
    <div class="head">
      <span class="tag">Lilo or Dia</span>
      <h2>Arc's successor, or Arc's ideas without the AI.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="feat-list two">
      <div>
        <b>Choose Dia</b> if you want an AI assistant built into the browser, on
        Chromium, from the team that made Arc.
      </div>
      <div>
        <b>Choose Lilo</b> if you want no AI of its own, a browser that is not
        Chromium, and code that stays open whatever happens to a company.{" "}
        <a href="/arc-alternative/">Leaving Arc</a> covers the move.
      </div>
    </div>
  </PageSection>

  <PageSection id="faq" layout="faq">
    <div class="head" style="margin:0">
      <span class="tag">Questions</span>
      <h2>Choosing a Mac browser.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-list">
      <details>
        <summary>Which of these is closest to Arc?</summary>
        <p>
          Zen, Dia and Lilo all offer a sidebar of tabs like Arc's. Dia is Arc's
          successor from the same company, on Chromium with AI built in. Zen is
          on Firefox's engine. Lilo is on WebKit, imports your Arc spaces, and
          brings back Peek, the Little window and link rules like Air Traffic
          Control.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can a WebKit browser run Chrome extensions?</summary>
        <p>
          Lilo and Orion both do. Lilo installs Manifest V3 extensions straight
          from the Chrome Web Store, and its
          <a href="https://github.com/Alexays/Lilo/blob/main/COMPATIBILITY.md">
            compatibility notes
          </a>
          list what still differs from Chrome. Safari only runs extensions made
          for Safari.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>What does Lilo not have that the others do?</summary>
        <p>
          Sync, mobile apps and a Windows version. Lilo has no account and no
          server, so there is nothing to sync through. Apple does not let a
          third-party browser use Apple Pay on the web, and passkeys from iCloud
          Keychain wait on Apple's approval.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Do I have to give up my current browser?</summary>
        <p>
          No. Lilo reads your other browser on your Mac and changes nothing in
          it, so you can keep both and see which one feels like home.{" "}
          <a href="/moving/">See what comes with you</a>.
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </PageSection>
</Layout>
